WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.336
-Normally I bring out
my whole family together,

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.438
but this year I am going
to start with my dad.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.640
All the way from his home
in New Hampshire

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.876
and his fresh
positive COVID test,

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.544
please welcome Larry Meyers,
everybody!

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.246
Give it up for Larry!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:16.082
-Hi.
-How are you?

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:17.784
Now, I know you do not
have any symptoms.

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:20.153
Are you still feeling okay?

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:24.324
-Yeah, it's kind of like being
busted, disgusted, and untrusted

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:28.227
when you -- when you get
a positive COVID test.

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:31.230
But there's two
good things about it.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.565
Three, I guess.

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:34.000
The first is that
I don't have any symptoms,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.602
and the second is that
I did test negative today.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:39.839
So I'm gonna be catching up
with you guys over the weekend.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:42.575
And the most important
is that even though

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:45.678
this is like you being in
the attic when you did the show,

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.014
I don't have to look
at the sea captain.

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:49.281
-Yeah, that's true.

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:51.050
You've always been
very anti sea captain.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.619
Let's bring some of the
sea captain's supporters out.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:56.589
My mom, Hilary Meyers,
and my brother, Josh Meyers!

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.524
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:05.565 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:12.572 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:19.612 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.381
Alright. So, first of all,
cheers, everybody.

00:01:22.381 --> 00:01:23.883
-Oh, cheers.
-DO you have your drinks?

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:25.618
-Oh, I got a drink right here.
-There you go.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.219
Cheers. Lovely to
be with you guys.

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.621
Happy Thanksgiving.
-Happy Thanksgiving.

00:01:29.621 --> 00:01:30.956
-And, Mom, be honest.

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:32.958
When Dad said
he tested positive,

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:36.062
did you think about staying at
home in New Hampshire with him?

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.231
-Uh...no.

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:39.599
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:43.369
-Dad, were you surprised
how quickly she left?

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.638
-Oh, no, not at all.

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:48.441
Actually, any time that I get
a little bit under the weather,

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.576
the first thing I do
is hide the car keys,

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:53.246
because I'm always worried
that she'll put me in a home.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.314
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:57.083
-That's very --
That's very wise.

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.885
Now, I don't know
about you, Josh,

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:02.388
but, you know, when I heard
Dad tested positive,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.056
that meant that Mom
was going to come here

00:02:04.056 --> 00:02:06.526
and get three days
with just us in the city.

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:09.128
And when I realized what
she was getting out of this,

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:12.232
were you worried like I was
that maybe she had poisoned Dad?

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:15.534
-She's had so many opportunities
to poison him.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:17.904
If she has,
he just is resilient.

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:19.839
-Yeah. Oh, this might be
a situation

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.008
where she has been poisoning him
for years and years,

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:24.210
but he's just built up...
-An immunity to it. Yeah.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.747
-Oh, that would be nice.
That'd be a nice family story.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:30.282
Now, of course,
you're not alone, Dad.

00:02:30.282 --> 00:02:32.718
You're with your Sheepdog,
Albert.

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:34.820
Is Albert good company?

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.722
-He's very good company.

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:38.791
He happens to be outside
right now,

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.927
even though it's dark.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:45.898
He's so popular and friendly
that everybody that comes up --

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:48.134
We live on the top of a hill,
on a cul-de-sac.

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:52.504
But every UPS man,
garbage man, FedEx guy,

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:54.240
everybody that walks up
the hill,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.908
every child that lives
on the street

00:02:55.908 --> 00:03:00.245
has to stop and play with him
and spend some time with them.

00:03:00.245 --> 00:03:01.747
So even the fact
that some people

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.383
might walk at night
with their dogs,

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.184
he's out there waiting
for them right now.

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:07.253
-That's really --

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:09.788
Now, Mom, you shared with us
recently your concern

00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:13.259
about how much Dad
is talking to Albert.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.161
-Well, it's beyond.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:16.796
-So what's a normal conversation

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.498
that you'll hear
between Dad and the dog?

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:21.500
-"I love you more than anything.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.169
You're the sweetest --

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:26.272
the sweetest thing
I've ever met."

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.140
He'll be saying this --
-Within earshot?

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.408
-Are you within earshot?

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:31.711
-I'm laying right next to him
in bed!

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.379
And I reach over.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:37.450
I think he's talking to me.
And I feel Albert's head.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.251
-That must be very jarring.

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:40.986
-It is jarring!
It is jarring.

00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:42.688
-Now, Josh, you are leaving
tomorrow morning

00:03:42.688 --> 00:03:45.190
to go back to LA to see --

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.792
to celebrate
with your girlfriend, Mackenzie,

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:48.528
and your dog, Debbie.
-Yeah.

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.629
-Now, Debbie is not
a purebred dog.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:52.899
-She's not, no.
-But she's very photogenic.

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:54.033
-Yeah.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.301
-Now, where did you
come across Debbie?

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.469
-We found her on the street.
-So she's a rescue.

00:03:57.469 --> 00:03:59.272
And not a rescue
you got at a shelter.

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.173
You were the actual rescuer
in this case.

00:04:01.173 --> 00:04:03.242
-Yes. Yeah. We found her.
Yeah, found her on the street.

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:05.244
-And they both dress up
very well.

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:07.280
Here is -- He's Mackenzie
and Debbie, who are --

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.748
-A couple beauties.
-Couple of beauties right there.

00:04:09.748 --> 00:04:11.851
Very lucky
to have you coming home.

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.320
We had a very nice trip
this summer.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.456
In August, we went
and saw our alma mater,

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:20.960
Northwestern,
play in Dublin, Ireland,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.395
and we went to a football game

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.697
at a beautiful, I should say,
Irish stadium.

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:26.532
But then something
happened, Dad,

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:29.234
that was -- It was as if Mom
wished it to happen.

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:30.736
-Oh.

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:32.671
-Yeah, this was
really something.

00:04:32.671 --> 00:04:35.074
There were 43,000 people there,

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:38.878
and it was a no-cash stadium.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:43.349
So you only could pay for beer
or food with a credit card.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:47.019
And 43,000 people were there
for this event.

00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:49.922
And the credit-card processor
goes down.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:53.759
So they had no way to accept
any credit-card payments.

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:56.896
So they gave everybody
free beer.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:04:59.064
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:59.064 --> 00:05:01.901
-And I should note, here's
a photo you took of mom.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.369 line:0%
And, Josh, what do you
call this technique

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.639 line:0%
that she's doing there?
-That is double fisting.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.141 line:0%
Double fisting. She was offered
a beer and she's like,

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:10.509 line:0%
"Yeah, I'll take one,"
and it was like,

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:11.844 line:0%
Well, you still have a beer."

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:13.279 line:0%
She's like,
"Yeah, I'll take one."

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.014
-I also should note,
looking at this photo,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:17.483
this is a very good way
for me to pose my chin.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.418
I think. I should do that --

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:21.320
I should do that
for my next round of headshots.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.255
Now, Josh, is it rare for you

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:24.991
to see your mother
double fisting?

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:26.625
Have you ever seen it since?

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:28.260
-Well, funny you should
mention that.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.896
As we arrived,
there was a soccer game,

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:32.865
the US's first soccer game
in the World Cup,

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.734
and we had some friends
who were at a bar,

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:36.268
and it was an open bar,
so I told Mom,

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:38.204
"Do you want to just come
straight from the airport

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.473
and meet me there?"
And she was like, "Yes, I will."

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:41.641
And she got a gin and tonic.

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.309
And then there was a nice guy
who's like,

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.278
"I'm going to the bar.
Can I get you something?"

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:46.813
She still had
half a gin and tonic,

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:48.881
but she was like,
"I'll take a gin and tonic."

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:50.582
There wasn't a long line
at the bar,

00:05:50.582 --> 00:05:52.384
and he came right back,
and I was like,

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:54.220
"Do you want me to put
one of those down for you?"

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.789
And she said, "No, I'm good."

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:59.091 line:0%
So this is just from --
That's from Monday.

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:01.160 line:0%
Double fisting again.
-Yeah.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.428 line:0%
-This is.
-Hey. Now, wait a minute.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.363 line:0%
This is an open bar.
-Okay.

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:05.832
-I think that explains it.

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:08.934
-Nobody worries that
you're bad with money.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.569
No one's sitting here
going like,

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:12.505
"Could she afford
two gin and tonics?"

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:14.640
You're missing
the whole point here.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.242
-When they're free, I take them.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:17.877
-Yeah, well, yeah. There you go.

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:19.378
In this economy, you gotta.

00:06:19.378 --> 00:06:20.879
-You gotta.

00:06:20.879 --> 00:06:22.181
-I will say my favorite --

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:24.450
One of the wonderful things
about being in Dublin

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:27.453
was how charming
our many taxi drivers were.

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:29.688
And, now, Mom,
would you agree that maybe

00:06:29.688 --> 00:06:31.824
one of the knocks on you
over the years

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.426
is that you ask
a lot of questions?

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.094
-Uh, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.862
That's a thing
that you've been told a lot

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:39.264
by the men in your life,
is you ask a lot of questions.

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:41.166
And, you know,
we love our mom very much,

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:43.702
and so we try not to be
too harsh about the fact

00:06:43.702 --> 00:06:48.207
that maybe the incessant
questions are a little tiring.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:50.743
But we got into a taxi
in Dublin,

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:53.679
and you slid into the backseat
and you said very loud,

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.348
loud enough for the cab driver
to hear, obviously.

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:56.715
You said,
"I have a lot of questions

00:06:56.715 --> 00:06:58.117
about what we're doing today!"

00:06:58.117 --> 00:06:59.418
And our Dublin cab driver --

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.353
This is within 5 seconds
of meeting my mom --

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:03.589
said, "I'm gonna tell you
what you're gonna do today.

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:05.424
Step one, I'm gonna
drop you off at a pub.

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:08.561
Step two, you're gonna stop
asking so many "fecking"
questions.

00:07:08.561 --> 00:07:11.063
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.565
He was like an Irish angel.

00:07:13.565 --> 00:07:15.534
-Yeah. Shut her down.

00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:18.070
-He shut her down with that.
You gave him a big laugh.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.206
-I did. I did.
-Suddenly he clocked that you

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.875
would be the kind of person
would enjoy a burn like that.

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:24.210
-Yes. Yes.
-Now, Dad,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.980
I know you and Josh golfed
a fair amount in Ireland.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:32.118
And I know you have a story,
a golf story.

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.487
-I've got an Irish golf story.
-Yeah.

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:38.357
-Well, yeah,
I -- I definitely --

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:41.326
I have a lot of golf stories,
first of all.

00:07:41.326 --> 00:07:46.098
And I think that one
of the best golf stories I had

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:49.268
was that when we were
in Ireland,

00:07:49.268 --> 00:07:51.870
I had never seen
a golf course before

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.738
because every time
I went there for business,

00:07:53.738 --> 00:07:55.674
I was always in the city.

00:07:55.674 --> 00:07:58.210
[ No dialogue ]

00:07:58.210 --> 00:07:59.778
-What happened?

00:07:59.778 --> 00:08:01.947
[ No dialogue ]

00:08:01.947 --> 00:08:04.116
What'd you do?
-I muted him.

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:06.752
-What?!
-Yeah.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:10.189
-I can't hear this story again.
I've heard it 50 times!

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:13.292
-You're not allowed to mute Dad!

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:14.827
[ Applause ]

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:18.263
-I'm sorry. 52 years.
I've had enough.

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:20.599
-I mean, he's having
a really good time telling it.

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:22.301
-He really is.

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:24.403
-Now, don't -- You guys
have heard this one.

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:26.005
-I don't know.
It's kind of more fun

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:27.640
when you just hear his face.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.308
[ Laughter ]

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:31.210
-This is story number 41
on my list.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.445
-You know what?
Why don't we let him wrap it up?

00:08:33.445 --> 00:08:34.814
We'll take a commercial.

00:08:34.814 --> 00:08:36.549
We'll be right back with more
from the Meyers family.

